testdata/modsettings/engine-2.0.77.dat is a mod-settings.dat FACTORIO ITSELF
WROTE. Nothing in this repository encoded it, which is the whole point: every
other test in internal/modsettings compares that package against itself and
would stay green for a reader and a writer that agreed on the wrong layout.

HOW IT WAS TAKEN, on 2026-09-07:

    "$FACTORIO" --version                     # Version: 2.0.77 (build 84539, mac-arm64, steam)
    FACTORIO_USERDIR=/tmp/fk-r5-settings ./scripts/run-datastage.sh
    cp testdata/tmp/datastage-settings-go-2.dat testdata/modsettings/engine-2.0.77.dat

The gate packages guest/go/examples/datastage as the mod fkd-example, whose
settings stage declares one startup bool-setting, fkd-enabled, defaulting to
true. The DEFAULT row's second run is the arm with no mod-settings.dat in the
mod directory at all, so the engine WROTE this file from nothing rather than
rewriting one of ours: 105 bytes, the version header 2.0.77.0 with a zero flag,
and the three sections with fkd-enabled true in startup.

WHAT IT HOLDS, and it was read before it was committed: the four version
numbers, the three section names, one setting name that this repository's own
example guest declares, and the value true. No path, no account, no machine
name and nothing from the Factorio install.

TAKE IT AGAIN, on this engine or another, by running the gate and copying the
same file. A line for a second engine is a second file beside this one, named
for its engine, plus its case in internal/modsettings; the format has not moved
between 2.0 and 2.1, so a second file is a check rather than an obligation.

MEASURED IN THE SAME RUN, and it is the strongest thing here: on the FLIPPED
row the mod-settings.dat the engine rewrote after the run is BYTE-IDENTICAL to
the one `fklua modsettings write` had installed (cmp, 105 bytes, exit 0). So the
encoder does not merely produce something the engine accepts; for this document
it produces the bytes the engine produces.
